To list all 200+ individual vulnerabilities that don't require user input would result in a massive, unreadable list of technical CVEs (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures). Instead, cybersecurity standards organize these into the official CWE (Common Weakness Enumeration) framework.
Here is the definitive breakdown of the major non-interactive vulnerability categories, mapped to their official CWE identifiers, covering the hundreds of flaws that require zero user input:

💻 1. Memory, Hardware & Architecture Flaws (CWE-693 / CWE-119)

These occur at the physical hardware, operating system, or memory management level without any frontend application interaction.
* CWE-1241: Hardware Side-Channels (e.g., Spectre, Meltdown) — Leaking data via CPU optimization artifacts.
* CWE-1300: Physical Injection/Fault Induction (e.g., Rowhammer) — Altering memory bits via electromagnetic or electrical disturbance.
* CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption — Memory leaks or CPU exhaustion over time due to poor background garbage collection.
* CWE-664: Improper Control of a Resource Through its Lifetime — Objects left open in memory after a background routine finishes.
* CWE-416: Use After Free — Referencing memory sections that the OS has already cleared or reallocated.
* CWE-415: Double Free — Deleting the same memory pointer twice, causing instant system crashes.

⏱️ 2. Concurrency, Time & Race Conditions (CWE-362)

Vulnerabilities triggered purely by scheduling, background tasks, or multi-threading timing conflicts.
* CWE-367: Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) — A file status changes in the millisecond after a system checks it but before it executes it.
* CWE-377: Insecure Temporary File Creation — Background processes dropping predictable filenames in shared system folders (/tmp).
* CWE-420: Unprotected Alternate Channel — Background system processes communicating via insecure local sockets.
* CWE-820: Missing Synchronization — Multi-threaded background tasks overwriting each other’s data simultaneously.
* CWE-833: Deadlock — Two background system services freezing because they are waiting on each other to release a file.

🔒 3. Cryptographic & Key Management Failures (CWE-310)

Flaws caused by mathematics, poor key generation, or automated lifecycle expiration.
* CWE-326: Inadequate Encryption Strength — Using outdated, weak algorithms (like MD5 or DES) that can be cracked entirely offline.
* CWE-295: Improper Certificate Validation — Automated backend API clients accepting expired or self-signed SSL/TLS certificates.
* CWE-327: Use of a Broken Cryptographic Algorithm — Implementing flawed mathematical functions natively.
* CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values — Predictable random number generators (PRNGs) generating guessable system tokens.
* CWE-311: Missing Encryption of Sensitive Data — Storing background logs or system configuration caches in plain text.

⚙️ 4. Insecure Configurations & Default States (CWE-16)

Vulnerabilities that exist purely because of how an operating system, server, or cloud architecture was deployed.
* CWE-1188: Insecure Default Initialization — Ships with administrative services turned on by default.
* CWE-521: Weak Password Requirements — Default system, root, or firmware credentials (e.g., admin/admin) left active.
* CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor — Debug modes or directory indexing left enabled on public ports.
* CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference (XXE) — Background parsing configurations allowing systemic server file read.
* CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource — Setting file permissions to 777 (read/write/execute for everyone) in the OS.

📦 5. Supply Chain & Ecosystem Weaknesses (CWE-1357)

Vulnerabilities inherited automatically from upstream code repositories, updates, or third parties.
* CWE-1395: Dependency on Vulnerable Third-Party Component — Legitimate apps running on outdated, compromised background libraries.
* CWE-494: Download of Code Without Integrity Check — Automated update engines downloading patches over unencrypted HTTP without verifying cryptographic signatures.
* CWE-1104: Use of Unmaintained Third-Party Components — Code relies on dead open-source projects that contain unpatched zero-days.

The Cyber Takeaway

If you are looking at the CWE Top 25 or database repositories like MITRE and NIST, you will find hundreds of individual sub-variants of these categories. They prove that input validation is only one slice of the security pie. A system can have zero text boxes and still be completely compromised via background memory architecture, bad timing, or bad configuration.
Are you compiling this list for a risk assessment matrix, or are you designing a system architecture and trying to figure out which automated scanners (like SAST or DAST) you need to catch these?
